WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(bright music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.270
- [Narrator] Some 600
miles east of Moscow,

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:06.180
a new facility in the town of Yelabuga

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.050
is getting ready to ramp up
Russia's production of drones.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.580
The White House says the plants

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.770
will manufacture Iranian drones

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.535
with operations starting
as soon as early 2024.

00:00:17.535 --> 00:00:18.750
(drone buzzing)

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.790
Russia's invasion of Ukraine showed

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.850
how inexpensive unmanned aerial vehicles

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.160
or UAVs were effective

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.090
on the battlefield for
reconnaissance and for attacks.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.000
Aviation experts say, unlike Ukraine,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.830
Russia's use of unmanned
systems got off to a slow start.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.410
Here's how Moscow is
catching up in the drone race

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.283
by building up arsenals of
low-cost explosive drones.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:46.410
(airplane jet revving)

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.250
Early in the war,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.540
Russia mostly relied on
conventional military UAVs.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.900
Reconnaissance drones like
this orlan have been effective

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.370
as spotting targets and
calling in artillery fire.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.100
But these systems are
relatively expensive,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.670
costing around $100,000 a unit,

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.530
and military experts with CNA

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.290
and American Defense Research Organization

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:14.370
believe Russian forces had a
limited number of these UAVs

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.867
with plans to significantly
ramp up production in 2023.

00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:19.700
(air whooshing)

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.380
Meanwhile, Ukrainian
troops started using cheap,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.530
modified commercial
drones for surveillance

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.520
and to destroy valuable
Russian equipment early on.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.460
As the invasion turned
into a war of attrition,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.230
Moscow realized its need for
low-cost explosive drones.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.230
In mid 2022, Moscow started
importing Iranian shahed drones

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.000
to targets Ukrainian infrastructure.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.450
CNA expert, Samuel Bendett,
estimated the price tag

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.200
for the shaheds to be under $50,000 each,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.260
which makes it cost-effective
for Russia to use.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.750
And with this new plant,
Russia will be able

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.920
to produce some 6,000 Iranian
design drones on its territory

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.500
in the coming years,
according to officials

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.776
from a country aligned with the US.

00:02:06.776 --> 00:02:08.010
(tense music)

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:12.482
But shahed can only navigate
to pre-programmed targets.

00:02:12.482 --> 00:02:13.620
(drone thudding)

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.710
So in response to Ukraine's
counter offensive,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.360
Russia has been increasingly

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.150
using domestically-produced lancet drones,

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.859
which are flown by pilots in real time.

00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:27.330
(worker speaking in Russian)

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.550
- [Narrator] In July,
Russian state media reported

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.880
from what it said was a former
shopping mall that was turned

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.730
into a plans to assemble lancets.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.340
Russia's defense ministry
has increasingly reported

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.610
on the small UAVs damaging
Ukrainian weapons,

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.335
including arms supplied by the West.

00:02:44.335 --> 00:02:48.752
(Vladimir Putin speaking in Russian)

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.780
- [Narrator] In August,
Vladimir Putin asked

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.750
for the production of lancets to increase.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.810
It's not clear how many of
these drones Russia has.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:04.980
Last October,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.507
Russian pro-war bloggers put
the number at 1,500 units.

00:03:09.507 --> 00:03:11.924
(calm music)

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.180
Kiev has acknowledged the challenges posed

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.120
by Russian drones such
as lancets and orlans

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.340
to its forces on the battlefield.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.800
The Ukrainian government
says the vehicles rely

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.910
on foreign made parts, some
imported from the West.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.430
The chief of staff to the
Ukrainian presidents call

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.280
for more sanctions on
Russian drone producers

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.550
and stricter controls
over components' exports.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.750
Military experts agree

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.270
that sourcing components is a major issue

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.310
for the Russian drone industry.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.800
But if Russia still
manages to quickly ramp

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.200
up production of low-cost drones,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.178
this could spell trouble
for Ukrainian forces.

00:03:49.178 --> 00:03:51.828
(air whooshing)

00:03:51.828 --> 00:03:54.245
(calm music)

